~ concrete evidence of Turing-complete computational power, not just a
  claimed label -- see tests/test_turing_completeness.py for the full
  reasoning. two independent landmarks from computability theory:

  1. the Ackermann function -- provably NOT expressible with only
     fixed-count loops, no matter how deeply nested. computing it
     correctly demonstrates real computational power beyond that.
  2. the Collatz "stopping time" -- its iteration count can't be known in
     advance for arbitrary input, so it requires a genuinely unbounded
     while loop, not a disguised for loop. ~

new vibes ackermann(m, n)
vibes on
  it's giving m vibe match 0
  vibes on
    sending vibes n + 1
  vibes off
  it's giving n vibe match 0
  vibes on
    sending vibes ackermann(m - 1, 1)
  vibes off
  sending vibes ackermann(m - 1, ackermann(m, n - 1))
vibes off

new vibes collatz_steps(n)
vibes on
  steps vibes with 0
  keep the vibes going n vibe doesn't match 1
  vibes on
    it's giving n vibe match 2 * int(n / 2)
    vibes on
      n vibes with int(n / 2)
    vibes off
    or not
    vibes on
      n vibes with 3 * n + 1
    vibes off
    steps vibes with steps + 1
  vibes off
  sending vibes steps
vibes off

manifesting "ackermann(2, 3) = " + str(ackermann(2, 3))
manifesting "ackermann(3, 3) = " + str(ackermann(3, 3))
manifesting "collatz_steps(27) = " + str(collatz_steps(27))
manifesting "collatz_steps(97) = " + str(collatz_steps(97))
